Considering the interesting catalytic performance of MIL101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) in the above imine reduction reactions, we decided to examine MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) for selective catalytic hydrogenation of the α, \(\upbeta\) -unsaturated imine compounds directly using hydrogen gas, which has barely been explored in both homogeneous and heterogeneous systems.12 Interestingly, for the substrate 7a, both imine and alkene double bonds were reduced in the case of homogeneous FLP, and no 7b can be observed after the catalytic reaction. However, the heterogeneous MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) shows a yield of \(87\%\) 7b for 7a under the same reaction conditions. This excellent selectivity could be attributed to the hydrogen bonding between the N atoms of the imines and the -OH groups from the framework and/or the interaction between the N atoms of the imines and remaining open metal sites.16 The α, \(\upbeta\) -unsaturated imine compounds with different substituted groups were employed to further investigate the chemoselective catalysis performance of the heterogeneous MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) . As presented in Table 1, the reaction yields catalyzed by MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) from the related α, \(\upbeta\) unsaturated imine compounds are \(100\%\) for 8a, \(91\%\) for 9a, \(81\%\) for 10a, \(100\%\) for 11a, and \(93\%\) for 12a. The high yields of these products highlight that MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) can serve as a porous FLP catalyst with excellent catalytic performance in the chemoselective hydrogenation reactions, which has not been achieved previously for any FLP-based catalyst.
## COMMUNICATION
On the basis of the reported homogenous FLP catalyzed hydrogenation reactions and the solid-state \(^{19}\mathsf{F}\) NMR of MIL\(101(\mathsf{C r})\mathrm{-FLP}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) results, the tentative catalysis mechanism of MIL\(101(\mathsf{C r})\mathrm{-FLP}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) catalyzed imine reduction reactions is proposed as illustrated in Figure S14. The process is initiated by the reaction between MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) and the imine substrate. The \([\mathsf{H B M e s}(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{F}_{5})_{2}]^{-}\) reduces the imine substrate and then converts to BMes \((\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{F}_{5})_{2}\) , while the MIL-101(Cr)-[LBH]+ converts to MIL-101(Cr)-LB. Subsequently, the MIL-101(Cr)-LB and BMes \((\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{F}_{5})_{2}\) react with hydrogen to regenerate the MIL-101(Cr)- FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) and complete the catalysis cycle.Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were employed to understand the tentative mechanism of the chemoselective hydrogenation of \(\alpha\) , \(\upbeta\) -unsaturated imine compounds. As shown in Figure 3, the unsaturated Cr(III) sites and the hydroxyl group in \(\mathsf{C r}_{3}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) trimers of MIL-101 can serve as "active" sites that interact with the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{N}\) group of 7a molecule. The calculated adsorption energy of 7a over the unsaturated Cr(III) sites and the hydroxyl group in \(\mathsf{C r}_{3}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) trimers is - 33.77 and \(-34.83\;\mathsf{k J}\;\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}\) , respectively, that is, binding between the trimers and 7a through \(\mathsf{N\cdots H O}\) and \(\mathsf{N\cdots C r}\) is thermodynamically favored, with the interaction between 7a and hydroxyl group in \(\mathsf{C r}_{3}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) trimers being slightly stronger (Figure S15 and S16). Therefore, different from the homogenous catalyst, the MOF catalyst can selectively reduce the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{N}\) bond in \(\mathfrak{a}\) , \(\upbeta\) -unsaturated imine compounds. These results support the experimental observation that the -OH groups and remaining open Cr(III) sites in MIL-101(Cr) preferentially interact with the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{N}\) group (rather than the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{C}\) group) of 7a thus to activate it, giving rise to the improved selectivity for the formation of product 7b.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were employed to understand the tentative mechanism of the chemoselective hydrogenation of \(\mathfrak{a}\) , \(\upbeta\) -unsaturated imine compounds. As shown in Figure 3, the unsaturated Cr(III) sites and the hydroxyl group in \(\mathsf{C r}_{3}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) trimers of MIL-101 can serve as "active" sites that interact with the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{N}\) group of 7a molecule. The calculated adsorption energy of 7a over the unsaturated Cr(III) sites and the hydroxyl group in \(\mathsf{C r}_{3}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) trimers is - 33.77 and \(-34.83\;\mathsf{k J}\;\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}\) , respectively, that is, binding between the trimers and 7a through \(\mathsf{N\cdots H O}\) and \(\mathsf{N\cdots C r}\) is thermodynamically favored, with the interaction between 7a and hydroxyl group in \(\mathsf{C r}_{3}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) trimers being slightly stronger (Figure S15 and S16). Therefore, different from the homogenous catalyst, the MOF catalyst can selectively reduce the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{N}\) bond in \(\mathfrak{a}\) , \(\upbeta\) -unsaturated imine compounds. These results support the experimental observation that the -OH groups and remaining open Cr(III) sites in MIL-101(Cr) preferentially interact with the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{N}\) group (rather than the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{C}\) group) of 7a thus to activate it, giving rise to the improved selectivity for the formation of product 7b.
In industrial processes, the recyclability and long-term stability of the catalyst are of utmost importance. Based on this consideration, we investigated the stability and recycling performance of MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) . MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) can be readily recycled with great catalytic performance, and the yield of the catalysis reaction can maintain \(100\%\) percent even at the fifth recycling experiment (Figure S17). The \(^1\mathsf{H}\) NMR data of the decomposed MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) in \(1.0\,\mathsf{\Delta W t}\%\) NaOH deuterium oxide solution after five cycles of catalytic reaction matches the original MIL-101(Cr)-LB, thus reinforcing the idea that the LB portion of FLP is competently bound to the Cr(III) sites. The
robustness of the catalyst was further confirmed by the well-retained crystallinity and pore structure in MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) after the catalytic reaction, as evidenced by PXRD and \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption studies, respectively (Figure S18 and S19).
robustness of the catalyst was further confirmed by the well-retained crystallinity and pore structure in MIL-101(Cr)-FLP- \(\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\) after the catalytic reaction, as evidenced by PXRD and \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption studies, respectively (Figure S18 and S19).In summary, we demonstrated the successful incorporation of a frustrated Lewis pair (FLP) into the MOF, MIL-101(Cr) with a tailored pore environment featuring open metal sites and hydroxyl groups in the SBUs. Such tailored pore environment stabilizes the FLP through strong coordination interaction between LB and MOF thereby rendering it with excellent recyclability as well as interesting catalytic activity for catalytic reduction of imines with direct utilization of hydrogen gas under moderate pressure. Moreover, the hydroxyl groups and open metal sites from the tailored pore environment within the MOF promote the anchored FLP with great performance in the chemoselective hydrogenation of \(\mathfrak{a}\) , \(\upbeta\) -unsaturated imines through the preferential interaction between \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{N}\) group (rather than the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{C}\) group) and the -OH group and remaining open Cr(III) site in catalyst, as indicated by DFT calculations. Our work not only lays a foundation to develop MOF-FLP as a new generation of efficient chemoselective heterogeneous catalyst, but also paves a new avenue for the design of precious metal-free chemoselective catalysts.
